 "interpretationRules": {
  "encodedRules": [],
  "sourceGuidance": [
   {
    "title": "SAS Scoring & Interpretation",
    "text": "The scale uses a 6-point rating scale for each item, ranging from -3 to +2, where negative scores indicate stressors and positive scores represent strengths. This asymmetric scale acknowledges that risk factors may require more nuanced differentiation than protective factors in child protection contexts. Items within each domain are summed to produce raw scores, which are then averaged to generate domain-level scores maintaining the -3 to +2 range.\n\nHigher positive scores across domains suggest family resilience and capacity, whilst negative scores highlight vulnerabilities and risks. The scale does not produce a total score, encouraging practitioners to maintain focus on the unique profile of strengths and challenges within each family rather than reducing complex family dynamics to a single risk indicator. This domain-specific approach facilitates targeted intervention planning and enables practitioners to track differential progress across various aspects of family functioning over time.\nDomain raw score ranges:\nEnvironment (9 items, 1-9, range -27 to +18): Providing a measure of housing stability, safety in the community, habitability of housing, income/employment, financial management, food and nutrition, personal hygiene, transportation, and learning environment.\nSocial Support (4 items, 10-13, range -12 to +8): Providing a measure of social relationships, regular services accessed, access to emergency services, and motivation for support.\nParental Capabilities (6 items, 14-19, range -18 to +12): Providing a measure supervision of children, disciplinary practices, support of child development, and mental, physical, and alcohol/drug abuse issues that might affect parenting.\nFamily Interactions (4 items, 20-23, range -12 to +8): Providing a measure of bonding with children, expectations of children, mutual support, and the marital relationship.\nFamily Safety (5 items, 24-28, range -15 to +10): Providing a measure of potential physical, sexual, and emotional abuse of children, child neglect, and domestic violence.\nChild Well-Being (7 items, 29-35, raw scores -21 to +14): Providing a measure of child mental health and behaviour, school performance, relationship with caregivers, relationship with siblings (if applicable), relationship with peers, and motivation/cooperation\nInterpretation guidelines for averaged domain scores:\n-3 to -2.5: Serious Stressor – indicating significant concern requiring immediate and intensive intervention\n-2.5 to -1.5: Moderate Stressor – representing notable concerns requiring targeted intervention\n-1.5 to -0.5: Mild Stressor – suggesting areas to monitor with potential preventive support\n-0.5 to +0.5: Adequate/Community Norm – reflecting typical functioning not requiring specific intervention\n+0.5 to +1.5: Mild Strength – indicating positive functioning that can be leveraged in intervention planning\n+1.5 to +2: Clear Strength – representing significant protective factors that buffer against risk\n\nOn first administration a two-way horizontal bar graph is displayed showing all six domain scores ranging from -3 to +2. On multiple administrations a line graph of all domains is shown to track scores over time.\n\n",
    "source_url": null
   }
  ],
  "reportScoringGuidance": [
   {
    "documentSha256": "c616f4907b20b6193c63b326a80a75e537cd67af6fdef3ff6dcba2433615eed2",
